= Integrating values and preferences with the best available evidence: A multi-criteria decision analysis approach
== OARSI World Congress, Toronto
#smallcaps[Date]\ May 2019

#smallcaps[Citation]\ #eval("Chua J, Hansen P, Briggs AM, Wilson R, Gwynne-Jones DP, Abbott JH. Integrating values and preferences with the best available evidence: A multi-criteria decision analysis approach. _Osteoarthritis and Cartilage_ 2019;27(S1):S308-9. doi:~#link(\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.joca.2019.02.705\")[10.1016/j.joca.2019.02.705]", mode: "markup")

=== Abstract
*Purpose*
Evidence-based practice requires that the best available evidence is
integrated with patients’ individual values and preferences, and also with
clinical expertise. Clinical practice guidelines (CPGs) provide
recommendations for interventions using consensus-based approaches that
generally involve weighing up the ‘best available evidence’, such as about
harm and effectiveness. Typically, broad consumer and health provider
engagement is constrained, and stakeholder preferences are not considered.
Multi-criteria decision analysis (MCDA) methods are one way of bridging the
gap between interpretation of evidence and stakeholders’ preferences. The
aim of this study was to prioritise interventions for knee osteoarthritis
(OA) in the early, mid and advanced stages of the disease in a MCDA
framework.

*Methods*
We used a 3-stage process to evaluate OA interventions by matching
stakeholder preferences for a discrete set of criteria, on which the
interventions were evaluated, with the best available evidence for those
criteria. Stage 1 involved determining the preference weights of 8
criteria, established in earlier research, representing their relative
importance to decision-makers: accessibility, cost of the intervention,
duration of the intervention effect, effectiveness, quality of the evidence
about the intervention, recommendation for using the intervention, risk of
mild or moderate adverse effects, and risk of serious harm. Each criterion
was defined by 2-4 levels describing the criterion’s best to worst
performance. The criteria weights were quantified through a MCDA survey,
using the online platform 1000minds.com, among a multi-disciplinary,
cross-sectoral sample of key New Zealand (NZ) stakeholders. At stage 2, the
performance of 75 interventions considered in the 2018 Royal Australian
College of General Practitioners (RACGP) OA CPG was rated on the 8 criteria
according to the defined levels. Performance was informed by the evidence
tables reported in the RACGP OA CPG with incomplete data supplemented by a
literature review and expert panel consensus. At stage 3, the total score
for each intervention was calculated by summing the criterion preference
weights associated with the rating determined in stage 2. Thus, the
interventions were indirectly evaluated by matching stakeholder preferences
(for the criteria) with the rating of the interventions on the criteria.
The outcome of this 3-stage process were rankings of interventions for OA
at each stage of the disease: early, mid and advanced. One-way sensitivity
analyses explored the effect of a change in one level of a criterion on the
total score of RACGP CPG recommendations for early, mid and advanced OA. An
interactive web-page was developed to visualise the scores and rankings:
the CMOR Evidence Table.

*Results*
The conjoint analysis survey was completed by 178 respondents (mean [SD]
age 53 [13] years; 114 female): 63 consumers, 79 health care providers, 24
policy-makers and 12 OA experts (16 [11] years relevant experience). Table
1 shows the preference weights of the 8 criteria expressed as percentage
points, where the maximum score (the sum of all criteria at their maximum
level) is equal to 100%. Rankings of 75 interventions for early, mid and
advanced OA were produced (Table 2). Of the 15 core intervention categories
recommended by the RACGP OA CPG ‘all land-based exercise’ is ranked 1st
(total score 74.6%) for early- and advanced-stage OA, but 2nd after topical
NSAIDs (77.5%), for mid-stage OA. Self-management and education (43.0%) is
ranked 15th at all stages of OA. Weight management (48.4%) is 11th for
early- and mid-stage OA, and 12th for advanced OA. Total knee replacement
(TKR) is 13th for early- and mid-stage OA, rising to 2nd (73.3%) place for
advanced OA. Sensitivity analysis indicated the Effectiveness, Quality of
Evidence, and Risk of Serious Harms criteria contribute most to score
uncertainty.

*Conclusions*
MCDA was used to integrate best available evidence with key stakeholder
values and preferences for knee OA interventions. The results suggest that
not all core recommendations for OA interventions are strongly aligned with
stakeholder preferences. Varying quality of the evidence about the
effectiveness of the intervention or the risk of serious harm may confound
the estimated total score of the interventions, and what constitutes a
meaningful difference in scores between interventions is subject to
interpretation. This method can enhance future implementation efforts by
threading evidence and stakeholder preferences together in a transparent
and robust process. The methods and preferences data may be applied to new
evidence, and to evidence tables of other CPGs.

